data: {"level":1,"reviewed":{"level":1,"label":"concept","text":"A k-way merge keeps one candidate from each sorted stream: the head, the smallest pair not yet taken. The\nsmallest of the k heads is the next output. A heap finds that minimum in O(log k), so the merge costs\nO(log k) per output, not O(k). Reading lazily means a stream is read only when its head has been output."},"hints_note":null}

